public class ArrayTools {
    /** Returns the value that occurs most often in a. If more than one value occurs the
     *  greatest number of times, returns the smallest of those values.
     *  Precondition: a.length > 0
     *  Example: mostFrequent(new int[] {4, 1, 4, 2, 1, 4}) returns 4 and
     *           mostFrequent(new int[] {3, 5, 3, 5}) returns 3. */
    public static int mostFrequent(int[] a)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns a new array of the same length as a in which the elements are those of a
     *  moved k positions to the left. Elements that move off the front are placed at the
     *  end, in order. The array a is not changed.
     *  Preconditions: a.length > 0, k >= 0 (k may be larger than a.length)
     *  Example: if a is {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, then rotateLeft(a, 2) returns {3, 4, 5, 1, 2},
     *           and rotateLeft(a, 7) also returns {3, 4, 5, 1, 2}. */
    public static int[] rotateLeft(int[] a, int k)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `mostFrequent`.

**(b)** Write the method `rotateLeft`.

sampleResponse:
```java
public static int mostFrequent(int[] a) {
    int best = a[0];
    int bestCount = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < a.length; j++) {
            if (a[j] == a[i]) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        if (count > bestCount || (count == bestCount && a[i] < best)) {
            best = a[i];
            bestCount = count;
        }
    }
    return best;
}

public static int[] rotateLeft(int[] a, int k) {
    int n = a.length;
    int[] result = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        result[i] = a[(i + k) % n];
    }
    return result;
}
```
In `rotateLeft`, the element that ends up at index `i` came from index `(i + k) % n`, and the remainder makes the index wrap around, including when `k` is larger than the array.

